    <div class="page-header">
      <p class="section-breadcrumb">Resources</p>
      <h1>AI</h1>
      <p class="page-intro">Artificial intelligence has become a genuine part of my research toolkit — not as a replacement for primary sources, but as an assistant for the surrounding work: organizing findings, drafting citations, transcribing difficult handwriting, and thinking through research strategy. Below are the tools, courses, and resources I've found useful, along with some honest thoughts on where AI helps and where it doesn't.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="person-section">
      <h2>This Site</h2>
      <h3>Built with Claude (Anthropic)</h3>
      <p>This website itself is a collaboration with Claude, an AI assistant I use for drafting citations from source images, building and maintaining the Eleventy site structure, troubleshooting code, and thinking through Evidence Explained citation formatting. Claude does not do primary research or verify facts — that work is mine, using original records and archives. What it does well is the surrounding scaffolding: turning my notes into properly structured data, catching inconsistencies I ask it to flag, and helping me keep a large, growing site organized. Every fact on this site is still sourced and verified the old-fashioned way.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="person-section">
      <h2>AI Tools for Genealogy Research</h2>

      <h3><a href="https://www.familysearch.org/en/search/full-text/">FamilySearch Full-Text Search</a></h3>
      <p>FamilySearch utilizes AI-powered handwriting recognition to transcribe and index a growing share of its previously unsearchable microfilm collection, making millions of scanned record images searchable by keyword for the first time. Free subscription.</p>

      <h3><a href="https://www.ancestry.com">Ancestry</a></h3>
      <p>In addition to Ancestry's use of AI to present potentially relevant records and tree connections, Ancestry has added a full-text search feature that searches through the text in books, stories and newspapers. Paid subscription required.</p>

      <h3>Transkribus</h3>
      <p>A tool built specifically for historical handwriting recognition, including cursive and non-standard scripts. Widely used by archives and researchers dealing with older or foreign-language documents.</p>

      <h3>Large language models (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity) as research assistants</h3>
      <p>General-purpose AI assistants aren't genealogy databases — they don't know what happened to a specific ancestor — but they're useful as research-strategy partners: what records would exist for a particular time and place, what an unfamiliar Latin or legal term means, how to interpret a DNA match pattern, or how to structure a research plan around a brick wall. Different tools tend to have different strengths (document analysis, transcription, conversational research planning), so many researchers use more than one.</p>
    </section>

